# hml multi-vendor index -- the machine-checked manifest for renode/vendors/**
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#   One row per vendor platform. renode/scripts/selfcheck_vendors.sh parses this
#   table and asserts that every field agrees with:
#       renode/vendors/<vendor>/firmware/vendor.env      (the build's inputs)
#       renode/vendors/<vendor>/firmware/platform/board_config.h
#       renode/vendors/<vendor>/firmware/platform/<linker script>
#       renode/vendors/<vendor>/<vendor>.resc           (the emulated memory map)
#       renode/vendors/<vendor>/<vendor>.robot          (which .resc it loads)
#   A disagreement in ANY of them is a non-zero exit from the self-check, so the
#   table, the platform descriptions, the linker scripts, the firmware headers
#   and the robot tests cannot drift apart silently.
#
# HOW TO ADD A VENDOR
#   Add a row here first, then add the vendor directory, then run
#   renode/scripts/selfcheck_vendors.sh and let it tell you what is missing.
#
# STATUS -- READ BEFORE QUOTING ANY ROW
#   NONE OF THESE ROWS HAS EVER BEEN EXECUTED. The machine this matrix was
#   written on has no Renode, no mono, no dotnet and no cross compiler, and
#   downloads.renode.io and github.com are unreachable from it. Every row below
#   describes what a real Renode + a real cross toolchain WILL be asked to do.
#   No row is a measurement. See docs/renode_multi_vendor.md.
#
# FIELD NOTES
#   uart_addr/uart_irq  the base and NVIC line from the part's data sheet, as
#                       recorded here. The UART is the image's only output
#                       peripheral and the demo takes NO interrupt, so uart_irq
#                       is decoration: it is pinned here and cross-checked, and
#                       nothing in this harness would notice if the number were
#                       wrong. Treat a wrong uart_irq as "unverified", not "fine".
#   uart_irq = "-"      no NVIC and no PLIC is wired at all. See
#                       renode/vendors/riscv/README.md for why adding one would
#                       trade a loadable platform for zero coverage.
#   heap_addr/heap_bytes  where the linker script puts the newlib heap. Every row
#                       here has heap_bytes == 2 * frame_bytes + 0x2800, because
#                       hml_reasm_open() allocates the two buffers AND its own
#                       reassembler context (sizeof = 9488 on 32-bit), and the
#                       self-check proves that arithmetic (see HML_VENDOR_REASM_CTX_BYTES
#                       in each board_config.h) and that heap_addr sits inside the
#                       SRAM window.
#   frame_bytes         the default --frame-bytes for this part. It is NOT 0 (the
#                       library default of 1 MiB) on any row, because no part
#                       here has the 2 MiB of external SDRAM the base platform
#                       models. 0 would still work -- malloc would fail, the
#                       image would print HML-MCU-DEMO-WARN and then FAIL -- but
#                       it would be a pointless run.
#   cross_prefix        the HML_CROSS_PREFIX a bring-up needs. The RISC-V row
#                       needs a different one: it is a different ISA with a
#                       different toolchain, and no single prefix builds both.
#
# vendor	machine	arch	elf	uart_addr	uart_irq	flash_addr	flash_bytes	sram_addr	sram_bytes	heap_addr	heap_bytes	frame_bytes	linker_script	startup	cross_prefix
